AB  - Progesterone biotransformation is worth studying because of the high industrial value of its derivatives. This study investigated the catalytic ability of the entomopathogenic filamentous fungus strain Isaria farinosa KCh KW1.1 to transform progesterone derivatives: 11-hydroxyprogesterone, 17-hydroxyprogesterone, 16,17-epoxyprogesterone and pregnenolone. In the culture of Isaria farinosa KCh KW1.1, 11-hydroxyprogesterone was effectively transformed into only one product: 6,11-dihydroxyprogesterone. Transformation of 17-hydroxyprogesterone gave three hydroxy derivatives: 6,17-dihydroxyprogesterone, 12,17-dihydroxyprogesterone and 6,12,17-trihydroxyprogesterone. Two products: 6-hydroxy-16,17-epoxyprogesterone and 6,11-dihydroxy-16,17-epoxyprogesterone, were obtained from the 16,17-epoxyprogesterone transformation. We isolated two compounds from the biotransformation medium with pregnenolone: 11-hydroxy-7-oxopregnenolone and 5,6-epoxy-3,11-dihydroxypregnan-7,20-dione. In this study, we observed only mono- and dihydroxy derivatives of the tested substrates, and the number of obtained products for each biotransformation did not exceed three.
